WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.842
[ Watch on KissFlix.co ]

2
00:07.112 --> 00:09.114
[theme music playing]

3
00:32.721 --> 00:35.975
[Captain Cook] The first hurdle on the way to becoming the best K-chef.

4
00:36.058 --> 00:37.726
[intense music playing]

5
00:37.810 --> 00:41.981
120 TEAMS 480 CHEFS

6
00:42.565 --> 00:44.984
WHAT IS THE FIRST DISH THEY MUST PREPARE TO BECOME THE BEST?

7
00:45.067 --> 00:46.735
WHAT IS YOUR K-FOOD?

8
00:46.819 --> 00:48.571
THE TEAMS HAVE 120 MINUTES

9
01:06.714 --> 01:08.924
[Captain Cook] One single dish.

10
01:11.552 --> 01:14.638
One dish to be cooked to perfection.

11
01:15.598 --> 01:19.143
The final eight teams to advance to the main round!

12
01:21.478 --> 01:22.563
[dings]

13
01:28.027 --> 01:30.404
The Table: K-Chef 2026.

14
01:30.487 --> 01:34.617
THE TABLE: K-CHEF 2026

15
01:43.292 --> 01:44.376
Welcome, Chefs.

16
01:44.460 --> 01:47.838
You fought your way through the grueling preliminary round to make it here.

17
01:48.422 --> 01:52.009
And welcome to the judges who will join them for the main round.

18
01:55.054 --> 01:58.265
And I am your host for The Table: K-Chef 2026

19
01:58.349 --> 01:59.975
and K-food ambassador.

20
02:01.518 --> 02:03.187
Captain Cook!

21
02:03.270 --> 02:05.898
-[laughing] -[audience applauding]

22
02:12.237 --> 02:15.157
The preliminary round was more intense than ever.

23
02:15.240 --> 02:19.119
The suspense never let up as we watched to see which teams

24
02:19.203 --> 02:23.165
would advance and which would be eliminated from our competition.

25
02:23.248 --> 02:25.417
I'd like to thank all the chefs once again

26
02:25.501 --> 02:28.963
for presenting us and our jury with such spectacular dishes.

27
02:31.131 --> 02:33.634
Whether you sadly fell short of the main round

28
02:33.717 --> 02:36.387
or fought your way through and advanced,

29
02:36.470 --> 02:40.724
I believe your love and passion for K-food are definitely second to none.

30
02:40.808 --> 02:43.769
Interest in the eight teams and their head chefs

31
02:43.852 --> 02:47.481
still taking part in the competition is higher than it's ever been.

32
02:48.315 --> 02:50.985
Oh, my mouth is already watering,

33
02:51.068 --> 02:54.154
just thinking about what incredible creations they'll serve up

34
02:54.238 --> 02:56.949
in the fierce competition ahead.

35
02:57.032 --> 02:59.284
[laughing]

36
02:59.368 --> 03:00.452
Now,

37
03:00.536 --> 03:03.580
we'll take a closer look at the eight head chefs who made it

38
03:03.664 --> 03:05.416
to the main round in just a moment.

39
03:05.499 --> 03:07.334
They're all highly accomplished,

40
03:07.418 --> 03:10.379
so I'm really looking forward to seeing what they can do.

41
03:11.797 --> 03:15.718
Well, it's about time. Shall we get this exciting show officially started?

42
03:15.801 --> 03:20.848
[vocalizing fanfare]

43
03:23.600 --> 03:24.643
THEME & MISSION

44
03:29.565 --> 03:32.067
The main rounds will be conducted in a completely different way

45
03:32.151 --> 03:34.069
from the preliminary round.

46
03:34.153 --> 03:36.280
First, unlike in the preliminary round,

47
03:36.363 --> 03:39.700
a panel of members of the public will take part in the judging.

48
03:39.783 --> 03:44.455
In other words, your food won't be tasted and judged only by the experts here.

49
03:44.538 --> 03:47.374
Members of the public connected to the round's theme

50
03:47.458 --> 03:49.626
will also taste and evaluate their dishes.

51
03:49.710 --> 03:51.295
One week before each main round,

52
03:51.378 --> 03:54.590
we're going to give you a theme for you to work on.

53
03:54.673 --> 03:58.302
Today, we'll reveal the theme for next week's first round.

54
03:58.385 --> 04:00.929
You'll prepare for the round based on that theme,

55
04:01.013 --> 04:03.140
so you'd better pay attention now.

56
04:03.223 --> 04:05.434
From ingredients to recipes,

57
04:05.517 --> 04:06.894
we'll be looking at how creatively

58
04:06.977 --> 04:10.022
you've interpreted the theme and prepared accordingly.

59
04:10.105 --> 04:12.316
On the day of the round,

60
04:12.399 --> 04:14.401
you'll receive a challenge based on that theme.

61
04:14.985 --> 04:18.030
As announced, you may feel free to bring your own secret sauces,

62
04:18.113 --> 04:19.531
as well as stocks and jang,

63
04:19.615 --> 04:21.825
but your main ingredients to be used in the dish

64
04:21.909 --> 04:23.911
must come from the pantry behind the stage.

65
04:23.994 --> 04:27.790
Also, the use of MSG is not allowed.

66
04:30.375 --> 04:32.753
You may not have a clear sense of what this means.

67
04:33.629 --> 04:35.047
Take a look!

68
04:36.215 --> 04:38.133
Rather than go on explaining,

69
04:38.217 --> 04:42.012
let us reveal the theme for Round One…

70
04:42.805 --> 04:43.806
right now.

71
04:46.934 --> 04:48.602
ROUND 1 ISLAND

72
04:57.402 --> 04:58.654
THERE IS AN ISLAND BETWEEN PEOPLE

73
04:58.737 --> 04:59.780
I WANT TO GO TO THAT ISLAND

74
05:11.041 --> 05:12.459
THERE IS AN ISLAND BETWEEN PEOPLE

75
05:12.543 --> 05:13.710
I WANT TO GO TO THAT ISLAND

76
05:19.007 --> 05:20.425
[no audible dialogue]

77
05:34.857 --> 05:36.984
[chattering]

78
05:38.777 --> 05:39.778
[laughing]

79
05:40.654 --> 05:41.989
Long time no see.

80
05:43.448 --> 05:46.076
JANG OK-SEON OWNER-CHEF OF ONGOJISIM

81
05:46.660 --> 05:47.870
[chuckles]

82
05:54.960 --> 05:56.295
Yes.

83
05:56.962 --> 05:58.630
It's been six years?

84
05:59.590 --> 06:01.133
You look very well.

85
06:02.134 --> 06:06.722
Oh, I heard you opened a new restaurant, but I haven't had a chance to visit.

86
06:06.805 --> 06:10.225
Don't worry about it. You've got enough on your plate.

87
06:12.269 --> 06:13.270
Right.

88
06:15.355 --> 06:16.690
Funny how things turned out.

89
06:16.773 --> 06:18.734
We were always competing against each other,

90
06:18.817 --> 06:20.986
and now I'm the one being judged.

91
06:22.362 --> 06:24.489
I suppose I should just call it fate, huh?

92
06:25.199 --> 06:26.200
Ok-seon.

93
06:26.283 --> 06:27.284
[sighing] Well, then…

94
06:27.951 --> 06:28.952
see you.

95
06:33.081 --> 06:35.459
[sighs] Isn't the theme a little too vague?

96
06:36.460 --> 06:37.753
If the theme is this abstract,

97
06:37.836 --> 06:40.464
won't it actually be harder to distinguish between the chefs?

98
06:42.925 --> 06:44.927
If they've made it this far,

99
06:45.010 --> 06:47.554
they should already have top-level ingredient handling skills

100
06:47.638 --> 06:48.805
and cooking technique.

101
06:48.889 --> 06:49.890
I think, uh,

102
06:49.973 --> 06:53.268
creativity and an understanding of Korean sensibilities

103
06:53.352 --> 06:55.854
matter more than technique in the main rounds.

104
06:55.938 --> 07:01.068
Think of it as a way to test the chefs' artistic qualities on a deeper level.

105
07:01.151 --> 07:02.152
Hmm.

106
07:03.403 --> 07:07.199
I know Ms. Seon is an excellent producer, but this…

107
07:09.743 --> 07:12.412
This isn't just an entertainment-driven reality show

108
07:12.496 --> 07:14.039
where ratings are all that matter.

109
07:14.665 --> 07:18.085
We have to create something no one's ever seen before.

110
07:18.168 --> 07:19.670
Don't forget that.

111
07:20.379 --> 07:21.380
[inhales deeply]

112
07:22.714 --> 07:23.715
Maybe…

113
07:25.008 --> 07:29.554
this is the vision we've been trying to bring to life for so long now.

114
07:32.015 --> 07:33.684
THE JANG OF KOREA

115
07:33.767 --> 07:34.768
ISLAND

116
07:47.823 --> 07:50.033
JOSHUA YEONG-HO PAYTON OWNER-CHEF OF HUSH

117
07:50.117 --> 07:53.203
[K-pop song playing on speaker]

118
07:57.416 --> 07:59.835
ISABEL DOMINGUEZ HEAD CHEF OF INTEGRAL

119
08:10.095 --> 08:11.221
[vendor] Oh, boy.

120
08:12.097 --> 08:13.307
The islands? [sucks teeth]

121
08:13.390 --> 08:15.517
What else would you get from the islands?

122
08:15.600 --> 08:17.561
All this fish comes straight from the ocean, see?

123
08:17.644 --> 08:20.480
-Right. I was curious, that's all. -[laughs]

124
08:20.564 --> 08:22.316
I was wondering if there might be

125
08:22.399 --> 08:24.735
special ingredients from a particular island, you know?

126
08:24.818 --> 08:27.654
-[chuckles] -Well, nothing really comes to mind…

127
08:27.738 --> 08:31.908
Unless you mean something like the skate dish from Heuksando. [laughing]

128
08:35.537 --> 08:37.497
POETS OF OUR TIME

129
08:40.250 --> 08:42.377
"A feast of light and shadow.

130
08:42.461 --> 08:46.631
Human life is a long journey between those two extremes.

131
08:46.715 --> 08:48.967
Light represents freedom and hope,

132
08:49.051 --> 08:53.388
while shadow is the by-product of the price we pay for bearing them.

133
08:54.222 --> 08:55.932
Fear, loneliness,

134
08:56.558 --> 08:58.685
frustration, anxiety."

135
09:03.899 --> 09:05.525
[voice sped up] "Human beings are the masters of their own lives,

136
09:05.609 --> 09:07.819
free to make their own choices, yet they must forever endure the burden

137
09:07.903 --> 09:09.613
of bearing the consequences of those choices.

138
09:09.696 --> 09:11.656
We strive to build meaningful relationships with others,

139
09:11.740 --> 09:14.534
but love and understanding are not the only things that arise in the process.

140
09:14.618 --> 09:16.703
Because communication is always accompanied by self-denial and doubt,

141
09:16.787 --> 09:19.081
we have no choice but to accept our fate as individuals among others,

142
09:19.164 --> 09:20.749
embracing the bad along with the good."

143
09:21.291 --> 09:22.876
[sighs deeply]

144
09:22.959 --> 09:24.711
THERE IS AN ISLAND BETWEEN PEOPLE

145
09:24.795 --> 09:26.671
I WANT TO GO TO THAT ISLAND

146
09:46.483 --> 09:47.484
[inhales deeply]

147
09:58.370 --> 09:59.496
Hmm?

148
10:01.331 --> 10:03.375
[chuckles]

149
10:07.712 --> 10:10.298
[whimsical music playing]

150
10:20.350 --> 10:22.102
[gulps, grunts]

151
10:22.185 --> 10:23.437
[laughs]

152
10:25.772 --> 10:28.859
[laughing]

153
10:30.610 --> 10:32.654
[chef] What could "island" mean?

154
10:35.198 --> 10:36.825
YANG JA-RYONG HEAD CHEF OF GOLDEN DRAGON

155
10:36.908 --> 10:40.245
Do they want us to make something with an ingredient from a particular island?

156
10:40.328 --> 10:41.872
Geez, my head is hurting.

157
10:42.706 --> 10:46.293
Something hard to get, something… unusual?

158
10:49.045 --> 10:50.380
That's exactly it.

159
10:50.464 --> 10:52.841
From now on, you must create the ultimate dish

160
10:52.924 --> 10:54.301
using seaweed from Marado,

161
10:54.384 --> 10:57.721
the southernmost island at the very tip of Korea.

162
10:58.305 --> 10:59.681
No, no, no.

163
10:59.764 --> 11:03.560
There's no way the challenge is that simple. Geez.

164
11:06.771 --> 11:07.772
Maybe…

165
11:08.523 --> 11:10.650
they want us to plate it in a way

166
11:10.734 --> 11:12.694
that evokes an island?

167
11:15.989 --> 11:17.032
Correct!

168
11:17.115 --> 11:21.286
Whoever creates the most island-like presentation wins this round!

169
11:22.078 --> 11:25.582
No. It has to be something hard to predict,

170
11:25.665 --> 11:28.251
something most people would never think of.

171
11:28.335 --> 11:31.046
This is so difficult!

172
11:31.129 --> 11:33.590
[whimsical music playing]

173
11:35.884 --> 11:37.052
Could it be…

174
11:38.011 --> 11:40.347
[Captain Cook] At last, you've figured it out.

175
11:45.227 --> 11:46.228
That's right.

176
11:46.311 --> 11:48.396
Island is just a metaphor.

177
11:48.480 --> 11:50.357
It's meant figuratively.

178
11:50.440 --> 11:52.108
Hmm.

179
11:52.192 --> 11:53.985
So that's what it is.

180
11:55.070 --> 11:56.071
What does…

181
11:57.322 --> 12:00.325
"there's an island between people" even mean, huh?

182
12:01.409 --> 12:04.788
An island is in the middle of the sea. Why would it be between people?

183
12:07.290 --> 12:09.125
Um, excuse me.

184
12:09.709 --> 12:13.088
-The VIP's main course is ready now. -What?

185
12:14.881 --> 12:16.049
Huh?

186
12:16.132 --> 12:19.261
The VIP's main course is all ready to serve, Chef.

187
12:20.554 --> 12:22.347
Uh, got it.

188
12:25.100 --> 12:26.309
So, shall I serve them?

189
12:27.602 --> 12:29.854
No need. I'll serve them myself.

190
12:41.241 --> 12:43.326
[gentle music playing]

191
12:52.669 --> 12:55.422
THE STRONGEST HOPE IS BORN AMIDST THE GREATEST DESPAIR!

192
12:55.505 --> 12:57.215
[alarm beeping]

193
13:09.060 --> 13:10.103
[beeping stops]

194
13:14.524 --> 13:17.611
[grunting]

195
13:17.694 --> 13:20.280
THE ISLANDS OF KOREA

196
13:29.289 --> 13:30.999
[footsteps approaching]

197
13:36.713 --> 13:37.714
Chris.

198
13:38.840 --> 13:40.342
Were you here all night?

199
13:42.510 --> 13:45.305
Why would you stay up all night doing this?

200
13:45.388 --> 13:46.514
[sighs]

201
13:46.598 --> 13:48.016
I underestimated this.

202
13:48.600 --> 13:50.560
I had no idea it'd be something like this.

203
13:51.269 --> 13:54.439
It's only the first round. I didn't think it would be this tough.

204
13:57.150 --> 13:59.694
This is tough.

205
14:03.365 --> 14:04.407
WANTING TO GO TO AN ISLAND

206
14:04.491 --> 14:05.492
"Island."

207
14:06.910 --> 14:07.911
"Island."

208
14:10.497 --> 14:12.791
"There's an island between people.

209
14:12.874 --> 14:15.001
I want to go to that island."

210
14:15.085 --> 14:16.586
There's an island.

211
14:19.047 --> 14:21.007
I want to go toward that island.

212
14:21.591 --> 14:22.592
Why?

213
14:27.013 --> 14:28.014
Chris.

214
14:28.556 --> 14:31.893
So when exactly did you leave Korea? What grade were you in?

215
14:31.976 --> 14:37.065
[inhales deeply] Um, third grade. I left after finishing third grade.

216
14:37.148 --> 14:39.317
You were pretty young when you left.

217
14:40.568 --> 14:43.863
I bet it threw you off when they gave us a Korean poem as the challenge.

218
14:43.947 --> 14:46.991
But I don't really know the poem "Island" that well.

219
14:47.575 --> 14:50.286
I imagine most of the chefs who were there didn't either.

220
14:51.496 --> 14:52.497
[scoffs]

221
14:57.544 --> 15:00.130
There has to be a reason they chose that poem.

222
15:02.716 --> 15:03.842
[sighs]

223
15:03.925 --> 15:07.595
It can't be something as obvious as using ingredients from an island.

224
15:10.598 --> 15:12.767
There has to be some kind of metaphor…

225
15:14.644 --> 15:16.563
[upbeat music playing]

226
15:31.453 --> 15:32.454
Let's go out.

227
15:35.290 --> 15:36.958
Let's go for a walk outside.

228
15:37.542 --> 15:39.335
Maybe to a park or somewhere.

229
15:39.419 --> 15:41.588
[scoffs] A park?

230
15:41.671 --> 15:44.674
Yeah, let's go somewhere open, get some fresh air

231
15:44.758 --> 15:45.884
and slack off for a bit.

232
15:46.509 --> 15:47.677
We can clear our heads.

233
15:48.803 --> 15:51.222
Let's… Let's just goof off

234
15:51.306 --> 15:54.851
just for a few hours and… and relax for a little bit.

235
15:56.227 --> 15:57.812
I'll go change quickly.

236
16:01.816 --> 16:02.817
"Slack off"?

237
16:04.110 --> 16:05.111
"Goof off"?

238
16:09.657 --> 16:11.326
[loud chattering]

239
16:26.925 --> 16:29.761
Wow, these look really fresh.

240
16:30.386 --> 16:34.224
Ma'am, you gather these yourself from around this area?

241
16:34.307 --> 16:36.392
Oh, of course.

242
16:36.476 --> 16:40.647
My husband picked every one of these by hand right out front this morning.

243
16:40.730 --> 16:41.731
Uh-huh.

244
16:41.815 --> 16:43.566
[vendor] Well? Pretty color, isn't it?

245
16:43.650 --> 16:44.943
-Yeah. -[chuckles]

246
16:45.985 --> 16:47.111
They look delicious.

247
16:47.195 --> 16:50.990
Well. Then take some. 3,000 won a bag.

248
16:51.658 --> 16:52.659
Wow.

249
16:53.493 --> 16:54.661
That's really cheap.

250
16:55.745 --> 16:58.498
Or should I boil some for you right now?

251
16:59.457 --> 17:01.251
[laughing]

252
17:01.334 --> 17:04.045
[peaceful music playing]

253
17:10.510 --> 17:12.011
[seagulls squawking]

254
17:43.585 --> 17:45.837
[phone buzzes]

255
17:45.920 --> 17:47.380
CHEF CHA

256
17:49.215 --> 17:50.675
Where did you go?

257
17:50.758 --> 17:53.928
I heard you took off somewhere after dinner service last night.

258
17:54.470 --> 17:57.557
I just thought maybe the sea breeze…

259
17:59.225 --> 18:00.518
would help me think of something.

260
18:01.102 --> 18:04.856
Of course, I don't think you would have given us such an obvious clue.

261
18:05.857 --> 18:07.066
[chuckles]

262
18:07.859 --> 18:09.193
So I thought about it.

263
18:12.238 --> 18:13.615
Aren't we all…

264
18:15.074 --> 18:16.159
islands in a way?

265
18:17.952 --> 18:20.538
An island doesn't have…

266
18:22.248 --> 18:24.375
to be something floating in the sea, you know.

267
18:25.335 --> 18:29.797
If you want to reach that island, but can't for some reason,

268
18:30.924 --> 18:33.051
you can still help someone else get there.

269
18:37.805 --> 18:40.683
[peaceful music continues playing]

270
19:10.421 --> 19:12.340
Chris Krantz…

271
19:12.423 --> 19:13.925
wer… wers… [stammers]

272
19:14.008 --> 19:16.552
Man, that's such a difficult name to pronounce.

273
19:16.636 --> 19:19.555
Anyway, people call him Chris K. Isn't that right?

274
19:19.639 --> 19:23.351
His father's German-American, and his mother died when he was young.

275
19:23.434 --> 19:26.646
So when was this guy adopted? When did he leave Korea?

276
19:26.729 --> 19:27.981
According to the records,

277
19:28.064 --> 19:31.234
he was adopted by an American father at the young age of 14.

278
19:31.818 --> 19:33.069
What was he doing before that?

279
19:33.152 --> 19:35.321
That's what we can't figure out at all.

280
19:36.114 --> 19:38.324
Our search has turned up no residential records,

281
19:38.408 --> 19:40.994
school or family records for him before eighth grade.

282
19:41.077 --> 19:43.496
What does that even mean?

283
19:43.579 --> 19:46.457
Did he just fall from outer space or something?

284
19:47.166 --> 19:48.167
I'll look into that too.

285
19:48.751 --> 19:51.546
So far, every school colleague we've spoken to says

286
19:51.629 --> 19:54.298
they know nothing about Chris K.'s childhood.

287
19:54.382 --> 19:55.675
The American father.

288
19:56.426 --> 19:59.846
We are currently trying to track down his American father.

289
19:59.929 --> 20:00.930
His American father?

290
20:01.014 --> 20:04.017
[sighs] But even that's proving difficult.

291
20:04.100 --> 20:07.645
[sucks teeth] We heard he lived in the US and then he moved to Germany--

292
20:07.729 --> 20:10.898
Ugh, how can running a background check on one person be this difficult?

293
20:10.982 --> 20:14.569
Is this all Pyeongyeon's intelligence network can do?

294
20:14.652 --> 20:15.653
I'm sorry.

295
20:15.737 --> 20:19.657
[sighs] Call our German office and have them look into the adoptive father.

296
20:19.741 --> 20:22.285
Not just him. Dig into the whole family.

297
20:22.368 --> 20:24.704
If there's anything to find, I want every last detail.

298
20:25.204 --> 20:26.247
Yes, ma'am.

299
20:26.998 --> 20:27.999
But…

300
20:28.624 --> 20:31.669
what kind of guy he used to be isn't what matters.

301
20:32.670 --> 20:35.256
What matters is how good he is now.

302
20:35.339 --> 20:36.758
That's what I need to know.

303
20:36.841 --> 20:38.009
Plus!

304
20:38.092 --> 20:42.013
His connection to Famiglia. That's extremely important.

305
20:46.434 --> 20:48.603
-[groans] Ah! That's hot! Shoot! -Oh!

306
20:48.686 --> 20:51.189
[yelps] It's hot! Shoot, it's hot!

307
20:51.272 --> 20:54.734
[groans] Did you brew this coffee in a blast furnace? Why is it so hot?

308
20:54.817 --> 20:55.943
"Did I brew the coffee in a blast furnace?

309
20:56.027 --> 20:57.111
-Why's it so hot?" -Yeah!

310
20:57.195 --> 20:59.530
-Hot coffee is supposed… Are you okay? -[gasping] Shoot!

311
20:59.614 --> 21:01.491
What the… Why does it taste like this?

312
21:01.574 --> 21:04.035
There's no aroma. It's just acidic.

313
21:04.118 --> 21:07.121
Is this really Guatemalan? Who brewed this today?

314
21:07.205 --> 21:08.748
Well, ma'am, Jeong took the day off.

315
21:08.831 --> 21:10.666
I'll make sure who's responsible is warned.

316
21:10.750 --> 21:11.834
Darn it!

317
21:11.918 --> 21:13.544
-Are you okay? -No!

318
21:15.088 --> 21:16.089
Dang, it's hot.

319
21:16.964 --> 21:18.216
Um, Ms. Baek.

320
21:18.299 --> 21:19.300
What?

321
21:19.884 --> 21:21.677
There's one thing that bothers me.

322
21:22.929 --> 21:23.930
What is it?

323
21:25.139 --> 21:26.474
Here.

324
21:26.557 --> 21:28.518
In one of the competitions listed here,

325
21:28.601 --> 21:33.022
Chris K.'s restaurant went head-to-head with Chef Cha Woo-jin's restaurant.

326
21:33.815 --> 21:34.816
Really?

327
21:35.483 --> 21:37.235
Where? Where? Let me see.

328
21:37.318 --> 21:40.696
Here. In Belgium. It was a competition in Belgium.

329
21:41.906 --> 21:43.908
WINNING TEAM AURELIA & HEAD CHEF JULIAN HAYES

330
21:46.744 --> 21:47.745
What?

331
21:48.871 --> 21:51.541
-Cha Woo-jin took second place? -Yes.

332
21:52.834 --> 21:53.835
But hold on.

333
21:55.128 --> 21:57.338
You said it was Chris K.'s restaurant.

334
21:57.421 --> 21:58.840
But his name isn't on here.

335
21:58.923 --> 22:00.550
Uh…

336
22:00.633 --> 22:02.510
Ms. Baek, I told you earlier.

337
22:03.094 --> 22:06.973
Chris K. has the unusual tendency to work exclusively as a sous-chef.

338
22:07.056 --> 22:09.851
The sous-chef of the restaurant that won this competition

339
22:09.934 --> 22:11.394
was Chris K.

340
22:12.812 --> 22:15.439
And the winning dish was his creation.

341
22:15.523 --> 22:19.110
[sucks teeth] So, then… [sighs] …effectively,

342
22:19.902 --> 22:21.737
he was the winning head chef.

343
22:22.655 --> 22:23.656
What?

344
22:23.739 --> 22:26.200
This is the one competition

345
22:27.076 --> 22:29.287
Chef Cha Woo-jin ever

346
22:30.705 --> 22:31.747
failed to win.

347
22:33.875 --> 22:35.459
Oh.

348
22:37.378 --> 22:38.838
Things are going to get

349
22:39.881 --> 22:41.757
more complicated than I thought.

350
22:42.675 --> 22:44.051
Famiglia…

351
22:44.135 --> 22:46.345
A guy like that joined Famiglia,

352
22:46.429 --> 22:50.308
and now he's saying he'll take us on and that he'll beat us.

353
22:50.391 --> 22:52.810
No. [chuckling] He's saying he can beat us.

354
22:52.894 --> 22:53.978
[chuckles]

355
22:56.439 --> 22:59.150
How interesting. [laughs] Huh?

356
23:00.568 --> 23:01.694
This is interesting.

357
23:04.989 --> 23:07.491
[gentle music playing]

358
23:19.378 --> 23:20.630
[groans]

359
23:21.380 --> 23:22.882
Oh, sorry. I'm sorry.

360
23:22.965 --> 23:24.133
I must've dozed off.

361
23:24.217 --> 23:25.468
No, it's fine.

362
23:25.551 --> 23:28.095
You were sleeping so soundly, I couldn't wake you.

363
23:28.179 --> 23:30.765
I'm glad you slept, even if for a little while, Chris.

364
23:32.558 --> 23:33.559
Was I snoring?

365
23:34.227 --> 23:37.772
Yes. You were snoring really loudly.

366
23:37.855 --> 23:42.235
You must have been exhausted. Like… [imitating snoring]

367
23:42.318 --> 23:44.237
Ah, I guess I slept well.

368
23:55.122 --> 23:58.042
[yawns] There are way more people than before.

369
23:58.876 --> 24:00.753
Yeah, it's lunchtime right now.

370
24:01.545 --> 24:03.089
Yeouido is always like this.

371
24:03.798 --> 24:07.093
There are so many offices here that it's usually pretty quiet,

372
24:07.176 --> 24:10.805
but everyone comes pouring out at once when it's lunchtime.

373
24:12.932 --> 24:15.935
Wow, they all look like they're in uniforms or something.

374
24:16.686 --> 24:19.814
-Wall Street is pretty similar. -[clicks tongue] Oh…

375
24:19.897 --> 24:21.607
Come to think of it,

376
24:22.233 --> 24:23.943
this place is an island too, right?

377
24:24.026 --> 24:25.903
-Hmm? -Well, I mean,

378
24:25.987 --> 24:29.198
Yeouido is a district sitting in the middle of the Hangang River.

379
24:29.282 --> 24:32.201
It's connected by bridges to both Gangnam and Gangbuk.

380
24:32.285 --> 24:34.245
It's not in the sea, but technically,

381
24:34.328 --> 24:37.331
it's still an island floating in the river.

382
24:41.585 --> 24:43.713
[magical music playing]

383
25:05.651 --> 25:08.070
[Han] Everyone looks so lonely.

384
25:30.509 --> 25:33.554
[Song-yi] Um, about that poem, Chris.

385
25:37.600 --> 25:38.601
Chris.

386
25:40.311 --> 25:41.312
Chris?

387
25:44.774 --> 25:48.652
-Yes? -The poem. "The Island."

388
25:49.403 --> 25:51.113
Oh… right.

389
25:52.073 --> 25:53.741
The second line says,

390
25:53.824 --> 25:56.202
"I want to go to that island."

391
25:56.285 --> 25:57.453
When you think about it,

392
25:57.536 --> 26:00.748
saying "I want to go" means you can't actually go.

393
26:00.831 --> 26:03.501
You want to, but you can't really go.

394
26:05.419 --> 26:08.631
Couldn't someone help them get there?

395
26:09.215 --> 26:11.509
Couldn't someone offer some kind of help?

396
26:13.386 --> 26:17.014
So, what if that someone were you, Chris?

397
26:26.065 --> 26:27.108
THERE IS AN ISLAND BETWEEN PEOPLE

398
26:27.191 --> 26:28.192
I WANT TO GO TO THAT ISLAND

399
26:28.275 --> 26:29.693
TIME PARENTS AND CHILDREN SPEND TALKING

400
26:29.777 --> 26:31.570
UNDER 68 MINUTES PER WEEK AVERAGE

401
26:32.780 --> 26:33.948
CHATTING DURING MEALS: 5 MINUTES

402
26:41.664 --> 26:44.750
According to a 2026 survey by Opinion Now,

403
26:44.834 --> 26:47.920
Korean families with children aged 12 or older

404
26:48.003 --> 26:52.091
talk to each other for an average of no more than 68 minutes per week.

405
26:52.174 --> 26:54.969
Of that, only five minutes on average

406
26:55.052 --> 26:57.513
is spent talking during the time of a meal.

407
26:58.722 --> 27:01.851
The government recommends reducing TV and smartphone use

408
27:01.934 --> 27:04.687
and increasing quality time spent talking together

409
27:04.770 --> 27:07.440
to encourage better parent-child communication.

410
27:07.523 --> 27:11.402
However, questions remain as to how effective these measures are,

411
27:11.485 --> 27:14.530
as they only encourage superficial conversations.

412
27:21.328 --> 27:25.416
The Table: K-Chef 2026's solution to improve family communication…

413
27:28.169 --> 27:30.588
is the dining table.

414
27:30.671 --> 27:32.381
[competitors murmuring]

415
27:32.465 --> 27:34.175
-The dining table? -[Han] The dining table?

416
27:34.258 --> 27:36.051
[Yeong-su] The dining table?

417
27:37.344 --> 27:40.556
Cameras 8 and 9, focus on Chef Cha Woo-jin and Chef Chris.

418
27:41.432 --> 27:42.766
Captain Cook, stand by.

419
27:46.228 --> 27:47.563
Ready the screen.

420
27:47.646 --> 27:48.647
Graphics.

421
27:49.523 --> 27:50.566
In.

422
27:51.484 --> 27:52.943
SIKGU

423
27:53.027 --> 27:55.279
[Captain Cook] Do you know what these characters say?

424
27:56.030 --> 27:58.699
Yes. It says "sikgu."

425
27:59.408 --> 28:01.785
Now tell me, what do these characters mean?

426
28:02.912 --> 28:07.458
Yes, they quite literally mean "mouths that eat together."

427
28:07.541 --> 28:09.251
People who sit together and share a meal.

428
28:10.211 --> 28:13.839
Now, look at the shape of this character on the right.

429
28:14.715 --> 28:17.343
It looks like a square table, doesn't it?

430
28:18.135 --> 28:21.972
We gather around it and gain the strength we need to get through the day.

431
28:23.057 --> 28:24.892
We eat food that gives us energy,

432
28:24.975 --> 28:28.687
and as members of a community, we exchange a great deal with one another.

433
28:28.771 --> 28:31.023
We celebrate happy occasions and comfort

434
28:31.106 --> 28:33.859
with one another, as well as times of sorrow.

435
28:35.986 --> 28:39.657
When conversation stalls, we take a break over something delicious.

436
28:40.407 --> 28:41.825
When we run out of things to say,

437
28:41.909 --> 28:44.954
the food itself becomes the topic of conversation.

438
28:47.414 --> 28:50.292
Today, we insist on communication within the family.

439
28:50.376 --> 28:53.379
But we're not quite sure how to go about it.

440
28:53.462 --> 28:57.049
We simply say, "Let's spend more time together. Turn off your phones."

441
28:57.132 --> 28:59.468
Instead of offering vague strategies like these,

442
28:59.552 --> 29:02.012
we'd like to propose a slightly different approach.

443
29:06.725 --> 29:09.520
Let's encourage communicating between generations

444
29:09.603 --> 29:13.023
by spending more time talking together around the dining table.

445
29:13.107 --> 29:15.776
And for that, this is our strategy.

446
29:17.903 --> 29:20.698
Bring the family together at the dining table.

447
29:20.781 --> 29:24.159
Connect the individual islands that make up a family.

448
29:24.910 --> 29:26.287
That will be your challenge

449
29:27.204 --> 29:29.790
for the first round of the main competition.

450
29:29.873 --> 29:31.208
What?

451
29:31.750 --> 29:32.751
That can't be right.

452
29:32.835 --> 29:34.753
-Do what with the dining table? -Huh?

453
29:35.421 --> 29:37.756
-[Geon-woo] Family? -[Du-yeong] Family connection.

454
29:37.840 --> 29:38.966
What is this?

455
29:49.101 --> 29:51.562
[Captain Cook] In the backstage pantry, you'll find ingredients

456
29:51.645 --> 29:54.565
Judge Kim Pil-hyeon personally gathered from the coastal rocks,

457
29:54.648 --> 29:55.858
along with other items.

458
29:56.817 --> 29:59.528
Choose your ingredients and head to your assigned kitchen.

459
30:00.279 --> 30:01.280
Your time limit is…

460
30:05.409 --> 30:06.410
three hours total!

461
30:06.493 --> 30:08.871
[intense music playing]

462
30:10.998 --> 30:11.999
[shouting]

463
30:21.967 --> 30:23.218
[chattering]

464
30:50.829 --> 30:51.914
[knocking on door]

465
30:52.623 --> 30:53.624
Come in.

466
31:00.047 --> 31:02.132
So? How was the first round?

467
31:02.216 --> 31:03.217
Well, so far…

468
31:03.300 --> 31:05.803
-Was it bad? Not bad? Okay? -Hmm.

469
31:08.972 --> 31:12.643
They have to come up with a dish that fits the challenge in three hours.

470
31:12.726 --> 31:14.353
I have no idea what kind of dish they'll make.

471
31:14.436 --> 31:17.439
[sucks teeth] Honestly, it does seem a little difficult.

472
31:18.691 --> 31:20.317
-What does? -The challenge does.

473
31:21.235 --> 31:23.112
Something about connecting islands.

474
31:24.321 --> 31:27.991
They're bringing in six families of four as guest judges,

475
31:28.075 --> 31:30.661
having them eat the food and then score it.

476
31:30.744 --> 31:34.832
I'm not even sure if that's really an effective way to evaluate food.

477
31:34.915 --> 31:38.335
The reason we invited ordinary families as guest judges

478
31:38.419 --> 31:42.631
is to see how they react to the food, how much it gets all of them talking

479
31:42.715 --> 31:45.718
and whether or not it actually piques their interest.

480
31:45.801 --> 31:50.180
The three expert judges will rigorously evaluate flavor and overall execution.

481
31:50.723 --> 31:53.016
Having two different kinds of scoring methods

482
31:53.100 --> 31:55.102
is for us to tell who comes out on top.

483
31:55.644 --> 31:56.645
What?

484
31:57.271 --> 31:59.773
-You already knew this? -[chuckles]

485
32:00.441 --> 32:03.193
I spoke briefly with Ms. Seon before filming started.

486
32:03.277 --> 32:06.029
Then why did you call me? I didn't need to explain.

487
32:18.208 --> 32:20.002
I asked you to come see me

488
32:21.211 --> 32:22.337
for something else.

489
32:27.718 --> 32:29.595
I really need your help, Ms. Baek Hyeon-ju.

490
32:32.514 --> 32:34.558
[upbeat music playing]

491
32:42.608 --> 32:44.943
The carrots are too thick. You have to slice them thinner.

492
32:45.027 --> 32:46.028
Yes, Chef.

493
32:56.580 --> 33:00.334
[Henri] Today's evaluation criterion is not simply the quality of cooking.

494
33:00.918 --> 33:04.880
It's the reaction of our guest judges, the families of four.

495
33:10.177 --> 33:11.637
[steam hissing]

496
33:11.720 --> 33:13.806
-How much longer? -I'll finish in under a minute.

497
33:16.058 --> 33:17.100
Okay.

498
33:32.366 --> 33:35.035
[Henri] Do not focus just on the flavor.

499
33:35.619 --> 33:38.997
This round, you must watch the families carefully.

500
33:39.081 --> 33:42.501
Notice how they enjoy their meal in each moment.

501
33:47.881 --> 33:49.925
All right!

502
33:52.094 --> 33:54.054
[Henri] What kind of conversation

503
33:54.137 --> 33:57.015
the parents and their children are having during the meal.

504
33:57.099 --> 33:58.100
And above all,

505
33:58.892 --> 34:00.644
focus on which part of the meal

506
34:00.727 --> 34:03.480
specifically makes their conversation livelier

507
34:04.064 --> 34:05.232
and more engaging.

508
34:12.364 --> 34:15.826
Please focus more on challenge interpretation and creativity

509
34:15.909 --> 34:17.578
than on cooking technique alone.

510
34:28.005 --> 34:29.506
Isn't this doenjang?

511
34:29.590 --> 34:32.551
Wow. The mood just got icy.

512
34:35.178 --> 34:36.179
I mean…

513
34:36.847 --> 34:39.224
doenjang in French and Italian cuisine?

514
34:40.767 --> 34:42.060
[Song-yi] Chris,

515
34:42.144 --> 34:44.730
what exactly are we going to do with this?

516
34:45.397 --> 34:46.398
What else?

517
34:47.149 --> 34:49.568
We try to win this in style. [chuckles]

518
34:52.738 --> 34:56.241
This is traditional doenjang. The aroma is too strong.

519
34:56.325 --> 34:59.244
I don't think it'll work in Western fine dining.

520
34:59.328 --> 35:01.622
It's not just traditional doenjang.

521
35:01.705 --> 35:03.206
It's something very special.

522
35:03.290 --> 35:05.125
[mysterious music playing]

523
35:08.045 --> 35:09.838
A bridge connecting the islands

524
35:10.631 --> 35:11.715
within a family.

525
35:13.634 --> 35:15.719
[Woo-jin] Six tables of family judges,

526
35:15.802 --> 35:16.929
three main judges.

527
35:17.012 --> 35:20.182
Nine plates total, with dishes from eight teams.

528
35:20.265 --> 35:23.018
Each dish gets about three to four minutes.

529
35:23.101 --> 35:25.520
If we want to get them talking in that short time,

530
35:25.604 --> 35:28.273
the food itself has to become the topic of conversation.

531
35:29.691 --> 35:32.152
Conversation starts with something familiar,

532
35:32.235 --> 35:33.737
something that brings back memories.

533
35:35.697 --> 35:39.242
To keep them from getting bored, the dish has to tell a story.

534
35:40.202 --> 35:42.454
A dazzling drama that keeps you

535
35:43.246 --> 35:44.498
on the edge of your seat.

536
35:47.250 --> 35:48.919
[Han] The key to drama is tension.

537
35:49.503 --> 35:52.005
A balance between the unfamiliar and the familiar.

538
36:02.099 --> 36:03.934
And the starting point is…

539
36:07.270 --> 36:08.271
curiosity.

540
36:14.069 --> 36:15.070
And…

541
36:18.073 --> 36:19.908
a connection with the audience.

542
36:26.039 --> 36:29.668
[Hae-seon] Chef Cha Woo-jin must not win this competition.

543
36:29.751 --> 36:30.794
What?

544
36:30.877 --> 36:33.588
Uh, but… Hold on, hold on. Um, uh, hold on.

545
36:33.672 --> 36:36.425
What exactly do you mean, Chairwoman? So…

546
36:36.508 --> 36:40.137
Cha Woo-jin is on our side, but he doesn't win?

547
36:40.220 --> 36:42.014
Then who, who, who, who then?

548
36:42.097 --> 36:43.140
[chuckles]

549
36:45.517 --> 36:49.271
Of course, this competition absolutely needs Chef Cha Woo-jin.

550
36:50.063 --> 36:53.775
We need Korea's biggest celebrity chef to generate buzz.

551
36:54.317 --> 36:58.321
And the longer Cha Woo-jin stays in, the better it is for the show's success.

552
37:00.282 --> 37:01.283
Listen, Ms. Baek.

553
37:02.159 --> 37:06.288
I want this competition to be Cha Woo-jin's last as a chef.

554
37:08.749 --> 37:10.542
As soon as the competition ends…

555
37:10.625 --> 37:11.626
[sighs]

556
37:12.294 --> 37:14.796
…I'm legally registering him in the family registry.

557
37:14.880 --> 37:16.465
-What? -I know

558
37:16.548 --> 37:19.885
that Chef Cha Woo-jin has been and will continue to be

559
37:19.968 --> 37:21.803
an enormous asset to Pyeongyeon.

560
37:23.263 --> 37:25.390
Of course, we've helped him a great deal too.

561
37:28.310 --> 37:29.728
But that boy means

562
37:30.312 --> 37:31.813
a great deal to me as well.

563
37:32.439 --> 37:36.359
Maybe the reason I've been able to keep working this steadily all these years

564
37:36.443 --> 37:39.029
is because I've always had him by my side.

565
37:39.905 --> 37:41.114
Ever since we first met,

566
37:41.198 --> 37:44.201
he's been by my side and done his best with unwavering loyalty.

567
37:44.868 --> 37:46.161
How much longer

568
37:46.912 --> 37:49.039
can I keep working like this?

569
37:49.122 --> 37:50.415
I'm tired now.

570
37:50.499 --> 37:52.542
Once this competition ends in a success,

571
37:52.626 --> 37:55.253
I'm stepping down as CEO of Pyeongyeon F&B.

572
37:56.004 --> 37:58.340
That's why I want to put a succession plan in place.

573
37:58.423 --> 37:59.508
As you know…

574
38:03.512 --> 38:05.097
I don't have any children.

575
38:08.100 --> 38:11.853
If the man who becomes my adopted son and inherits the company

576
38:11.937 --> 38:13.438
wins the competition we're hosting,

577
38:13.522 --> 38:15.065
it'll immediately raise questions

578
38:15.148 --> 38:17.317
-about the competition's fairness. -[scoffs]

579
38:17.400 --> 38:20.695
And public opinion of Pyeongyeon Group as a whole will turn negative again.

580
38:21.613 --> 38:23.323
Ms. Seon is excellent at her job,

581
38:23.406 --> 38:25.450
but she's too much of a stickler for the rules,

582
38:25.534 --> 38:26.868
almost to a fault.

583
38:33.667 --> 38:35.544
And that is why I need your help.

584
38:37.045 --> 38:38.755
You know what I mean, don't you?

585
38:40.298 --> 38:42.175
He cannot win.

586
38:43.510 --> 38:46.680
Cha Woo-jin simply cannot win.

587
38:47.514 --> 38:49.391
Then he'll become her successor.

588
38:50.600 --> 38:53.395
She wants to make the competition a success

589
38:53.478 --> 38:54.729
and save the company.

590
38:55.397 --> 38:57.399
Retire in style, boost her image

591
38:57.482 --> 39:01.653
and even leave Pyeongyeon in the hands of a successor who's exactly to her liking.

592
39:03.196 --> 39:05.157
The drunkard's intention is not in the wine.

593
39:05.240 --> 39:06.908
IDIOM - WHEN SOMEONE HAS AN ULTERIOR MOTIVE

594
39:06.992 --> 39:09.411
What a little snake. Damn it.

595
39:10.704 --> 39:12.581
Is something wrong, Ms. Baek?

596
39:12.664 --> 39:13.915
Oh, it's nothing.

597
39:14.791 --> 39:18.211
We're doing all the work just to hand the prize over to someone else.

598
39:20.130 --> 39:21.548
You have one minute.

599
39:21.631 --> 39:22.716
One minute remaining.

600
39:22.799 --> 39:25.427
Chefs, please begin wrapping up.

601
39:34.853 --> 39:37.063
[upbeat music playing]

602
40:34.371 --> 40:36.456
[Captain Cook] Attention, Chefs! Attention, Chefs!

603
40:36.539 --> 40:38.500
You have exactly ten seconds.

604
40:39.084 --> 40:40.418
Ten.

605
40:41.253 --> 40:42.629
Nine.

606
40:42.712 --> 40:44.839
-[bells ding] -Eight.

607
40:44.923 --> 40:46.216
Seven.

608
40:47.217 --> 40:48.593
Six.

609
40:49.928 --> 40:51.263
Five.

610
40:51.346 --> 40:53.181
Four.

611
40:53.807 --> 40:55.100
Three.

612
40:55.183 --> 40:56.184
Two.

613
40:56.810 --> 40:58.061
One.

614
40:58.144 --> 41:00.063
Your time is now up.

615
41:00.146 --> 41:03.900
Chefs, take your hands off everything and step away from your kitchen tables.

616
41:03.984 --> 41:08.113
And now, in the order you rang your bells, allow me to introduce our team,

617
41:08.196 --> 41:10.365
who will serve your finished dishes!

618
41:10.448 --> 41:13.076
[dramatic music playing]

619
41:27.090 --> 41:28.842
THE TABLE: K-CHEF 2026

620
41:30.802 --> 41:33.346
I deliberately tried not to overcomplicate things.

621
41:33.972 --> 41:38.601
I focused on one thing, satisfying both parents and children.

622
41:38.685 --> 41:39.811
[chopping]

623
41:50.113 --> 41:52.907
It's hard to bridge a 30-year generation gap

624
41:52.991 --> 41:54.826
with the same tastes.

625
41:56.369 --> 41:59.122
So I made half the dish for one generation

626
41:59.205 --> 42:01.041
and the other half for the next.

627
42:02.125 --> 42:06.796
The important part was capturing the feel of each generation.

628
42:08.840 --> 42:10.633
-[murmuring] -What is that?

629
42:10.717 --> 42:12.427
The soup's exquisite.

630
42:16.806 --> 42:18.516
[quiet chattering]

631
42:23.438 --> 42:24.647
Peru, where I was born,

632
42:24.731 --> 42:26.274
has a rich tradition of freshwater and seafood dishes.

633
42:27.442 --> 42:30.528
I combined a variety of seafood and seasonings

634
42:30.612 --> 42:32.280
to evoke the feeling of an island.

635
42:32.364 --> 42:34.908
The technique is mainly South American,

636
42:34.991 --> 42:36.284
but the roots of the dish

637
42:36.993 --> 42:37.994
are Korean.

638
42:39.871 --> 42:41.623
Because my mother is Korean.

639
42:42.290 --> 42:43.291
[chuckles]

640
42:59.474 --> 43:01.267
JOSHUA YEONG-HO PAYTON OWNER-CHEF, HUSH

641
43:01.351 --> 43:03.478
I didn't focus too heavily on seafood as an ingredient.

642
43:03.561 --> 43:06.439
Instead, I thought a lot about one question.

643
43:06.523 --> 43:08.566
"How can I get families to enjoy talking

644
43:09.359 --> 43:10.985
while they eat my food?"

645
43:11.069 --> 43:14.072
Because the challenge was about family communication.

646
43:16.866 --> 43:18.827
-It's enough, Chef? -Yeah.

647
43:18.910 --> 43:20.787
-Bring me the sauce. -Yes, Chef.

648
43:20.870 --> 43:22.747
[Joshua] I focused more on

649
43:22.831 --> 43:24.916
texture than on flavor and aroma.

650
43:25.625 --> 43:27.794
The enjoyment you get from chewing.

651
43:29.129 --> 43:32.298
Different textures appeal to everyone.

652
43:33.633 --> 43:36.553
Children are especially attuned to that.

653
43:45.687 --> 43:47.147
YOO IN-SEOP HEAD CHEF, SEOP

654
43:47.230 --> 43:48.565
Uh, since it's the first round,

655
43:48.648 --> 43:52.694
I started by asking myself, "What is the spirit of Korean cuisine?"

656
43:52.777 --> 43:55.822
Not some old-fashioned notion of tradition,

657
43:55.905 --> 43:57.866
but more in a way of… [inhales]

658
43:57.949 --> 44:00.994
…uh, let's see… the spirit. [chuckles]

659
44:01.077 --> 44:02.704
Yes, the spirit of it all.

660
44:02.787 --> 44:04.289
JANG OK-SEON OWNER-CHEF OF ONGOJISIM

661
44:04.372 --> 44:06.749
The essence of Korean food comes from a mother's heart.

662
44:06.833 --> 44:09.002
Everything originates from there.

663
44:09.085 --> 44:10.211
Well, my…

664
44:10.295 --> 44:12.005
PARK JE-HYEON OWNER-CHEF OF BINGSAK

665
44:12.088 --> 44:14.007
My youngest daughter is very ill.

666
44:14.090 --> 44:17.343
She's the person that I love most in this world.

667
44:19.512 --> 44:20.972
I thought about which of the foods

668
44:21.055 --> 44:24.642
that I'd want my daughter to eat with a smile on her face.

669
44:25.560 --> 44:27.312
[In-seop] With that in mind…

670
44:27.395 --> 44:29.272
[Ok-seon] With that in mind…

671
44:30.523 --> 44:32.317
With that in mind, I made this dish.

672
44:47.540 --> 44:48.750
ROUND 1 SCORE CARD

673
44:54.756 --> 44:56.382
[chef] I'm confident.

674
44:56.466 --> 44:58.134
YANG JA-RYONG HEAD CHEF OF GOLDEN DRAGON

675
44:58.218 --> 44:59.302
All the way to the final round,

676
44:59.385 --> 45:02.180
I'll show you dishes that exceed your expectations.

677
45:03.097 --> 45:04.599
You can bet on that.

678
45:07.435 --> 45:09.771
[Captain Cook] The seventh dish we're presenting to you today

679
45:10.897 --> 45:12.899
is by Exception. Let's have a look.

680
45:13.983 --> 45:16.319
Please reveal your dish.

681
45:19.614 --> 45:22.242
[quiet chattering]

682
45:26.037 --> 45:29.165
[Woo-jin] It's chicken breast filled with a custard-like mixture

683
45:29.249 --> 45:30.750
made with egg meringue.

684
45:31.751 --> 45:34.796
The tender texture of wine-braised chicken breast

685
45:35.547 --> 45:38.216
and the sweet custard puffed up inside

686
45:38.299 --> 45:41.636
will be as fun to look at as it is to eat.

687
45:42.303 --> 45:47.141
This dish was actually inspired by a famous confectionery product.

688
45:47.225 --> 45:49.769
It's that snack you all love and enjoy.

689
45:51.187 --> 45:53.147
[chuckles] That's right.

690
45:54.899 --> 45:57.443
I wanted to create something that looks familiar,

691
45:57.527 --> 46:00.947
while elevating its flavor as much as I could.

692
46:01.573 --> 46:04.117
I slightly reshaped the chicken breast.

693
46:04.200 --> 46:06.202
Hmm… I'm not sure how well it turned out,

694
46:06.828 --> 46:10.331
but I shaped it to resemble an island off in the West Sea.

695
46:10.415 --> 46:13.835
It's where I grew up as a child.

696
46:14.669 --> 46:18.256
The custard inside the chicken breast consists of 12 ingredients.

697
46:18.339 --> 46:21.884
I tried to make each ingredient come through

698
46:21.968 --> 46:23.678
as distinctly as possible,

699
46:23.761 --> 46:27.056
so I'd like the parents and children to try and guess what's in there.

700
46:27.807 --> 46:30.435
I hope this serves as a fun experience.

701
46:30.518 --> 46:33.313
[audience applauding]

702
46:51.581 --> 46:53.499
[Henri] Chicken breast.

703
46:53.583 --> 46:56.044
A normally plain, bland ingredient.

704
46:56.711 --> 46:57.712
However,

705
46:57.795 --> 46:59.380
the oozing layered custard

706
46:59.464 --> 47:02.884
provides children with a taste similar to a sweet dessert.

707
47:02.967 --> 47:04.510
Simple,

708
47:04.594 --> 47:05.803
but sophisticated.

709
47:06.679 --> 47:09.098
Like a well-made drama.

710
47:10.975 --> 47:13.978
[director] Wow, this is killing me. I'm hungry. How can I work like this?

711
47:14.062 --> 47:15.563
Seriously. Dinner's right around the corner.

712
47:15.647 --> 47:17.899
Regular food isn't gonna look appetizing after this.

713
47:17.982 --> 47:20.318
Oh, Ms. Seon, it's our first team dinner today.

714
47:20.401 --> 47:23.655
You'd better treat us to something really good. [chuckling]

715
47:29.035 --> 47:30.078
It's nice and light.

716
47:32.622 --> 47:35.375
[Captain Cook] Now it's time for our final team.

717
47:35.458 --> 47:37.293
Today's final dish

718
47:37.377 --> 47:39.671
is from Team Famiglia.

719
48:15.498 --> 48:20.336
Judges, you've tasted many excellent dishes so far tonight.

720
48:22.171 --> 48:26.926
Now, it's time for the final dish of Round One.

721
48:27.009 --> 48:29.053
[mysterious music playing]

722
48:47.947 --> 48:51.033
[Captain Cook] The final dish of the day, created by Team Famiglia.

723
48:51.117 --> 48:52.243
Please reveal your dish!

724
48:55.788 --> 48:56.789
[diner gasps]

725
48:56.873 --> 48:57.874
Wow!

726
49:02.462 --> 49:05.006
[emotional music playing]

727
49:13.848 --> 49:15.767
[murmuring]

728
49:30.031 --> 49:32.283
[Henri] Doenjang and rice.

729
49:32.366 --> 49:35.495
Abalone and finely chopped miyeokgwi.

730
49:35.578 --> 49:38.164
He first made rice with miyeokgwi and doenjang

731
49:38.873 --> 49:40.750
and then he turned it into ravioli.

732
49:42.293 --> 49:43.503
Hmm.

733
49:43.586 --> 49:45.004
But not ordinary rice.

734
49:46.506 --> 49:47.924
Something different.

735
49:48.841 --> 49:50.343
What did he put in the dough?

736
49:51.803 --> 49:54.639
The aroma is so intense.

737
49:55.181 --> 49:56.557
And the sauce,

738
49:57.433 --> 49:59.101
not authentically Korean.

739
49:59.185 --> 50:02.396
It's far more complex.

740
50:04.524 --> 50:05.691
Seashore.

741
50:06.776 --> 50:09.028
Gentle waves surrounding me.

742
50:10.488 --> 50:11.489
Something

743
50:12.323 --> 50:13.324
overwhelming.

744
50:15.660 --> 50:16.661
Wait.

745
50:18.496 --> 50:19.831
Connected.

746
50:19.914 --> 50:24.043
All connected with thin lines which are almost invisible.

747
50:24.794 --> 50:25.795
Like

748
50:26.879 --> 50:28.047
cheonggukjang.

749
50:28.130 --> 50:30.299
[mysterious music continues playing]

750
50:46.899 --> 50:48.651
[dramatic music playing]

751
51:10.089 --> 51:13.301
[Han] Doenjang is one of Korea's signature ingredients.

752
51:14.385 --> 51:16.929
It's like a neighbor, both familiar and distant.

753
51:17.930 --> 51:21.392
The older generation is used to doenjang's rich, earthy smell,

754
51:21.475 --> 51:23.060
but younger generations,

755
51:23.144 --> 51:24.896
despite knowing it's healthy,

756
51:24.979 --> 51:27.315
often dislike it because of the smell.

757
51:28.900 --> 51:31.986
You could say, it's like the teachings of your parents.

758
51:32.069 --> 51:34.363
You know they're right, but it still feels old-fashioned.

759
51:34.447 --> 51:35.781
[gentle laughing]

760
51:35.865 --> 51:38.200
This isn't commercially produced doenjang.

761
51:38.993 --> 51:42.121
It was actually made by a woman in Gangjin.

762
51:42.914 --> 51:46.208
While fermenting the meju, she adds rice bran.

763
51:46.792 --> 51:49.545
Doing so preserves the deep flavor of the doenjang,

764
51:49.629 --> 51:52.465
while removing the earthy smell and enhancing the aroma.

765
51:52.548 --> 51:54.258
I used this doenjang

766
51:54.342 --> 51:57.136
with various vegetables to make gangdoenjang.

767
51:57.219 --> 52:00.640
Then I incorporated that into an Italian dish.

768
52:00.723 --> 52:03.392
I hoped the doenjang would make unfamiliar Western food

769
52:03.476 --> 52:05.519
feel more approachable to the older generation.

770
52:05.603 --> 52:08.105
Meanwhile, the ravioli's distinctive flavor and aroma

771
52:08.189 --> 52:10.274
would pique the younger generation's curiosity

772
52:10.358 --> 52:11.984
and get them talking to each other.

773
52:13.653 --> 52:15.321
Because curiosity

774
52:15.404 --> 52:16.447
is the first step

775
52:17.531 --> 52:18.866
toward getting closer.

776
52:20.743 --> 52:22.954
Please take a closer look at the dish.

777
52:26.749 --> 52:30.711
The barely visible threads connecting the ravioli represent

778
52:31.462 --> 52:32.880
the hope I have.

779
52:35.967 --> 52:38.511
We may forget it in our daily lives,

780
52:39.720 --> 52:40.972
but the truth is

781
52:41.055 --> 52:43.349
we're all connected by an invisible thread.

782
52:45.726 --> 52:48.145
A thread that's called longing.

783
52:49.730 --> 52:52.358
I wanted to remind you of its importance.

784
53:00.366 --> 53:05.871
When doenjang is used in Western cuisine, people often use Japanese white miso.

785
53:05.955 --> 53:09.041
But the flavor and aroma of Korean doenjang

786
53:09.709 --> 53:13.546
is so deep and rich that nothing else compares to that flavor.

787
53:14.296 --> 53:17.133
If its distinctive aroma makes it difficult to pair

788
53:17.216 --> 53:18.384
with Western ingredients,

789
53:18.467 --> 53:20.678
then it's up to chefs like me

790
53:20.761 --> 53:23.889
to help the two communicate with each other.

791
53:32.523 --> 53:35.568
After all, isn't that also a way

792
53:35.651 --> 53:37.611
of connecting "islands" that want…

793
53:39.363 --> 53:40.781
to move closer but can't?

794
53:43.492 --> 53:45.161
[San] Do you know how much deeper and richer

795
53:45.244 --> 53:48.456
our traditional doenjang is than Japanese white miso?

796
53:49.165 --> 53:50.791
Saying Korean doenjang

797
53:50.875 --> 53:53.753
can't be used in Western cuisine because of its aroma is an excuse.

798
53:53.836 --> 53:55.755
What chefs like us should do

799
53:56.422 --> 53:58.174
is help the two communicate.

800
54:02.094 --> 54:04.764
[Henri] We will now announce the results of Round One.

801
54:06.057 --> 54:07.892
The team that's been eliminated…

802
54:07.975 --> 54:10.478
[tense music playing]

803
54:22.782 --> 54:23.783
Unfortunately,

804
54:24.575 --> 54:26.660
it's Chef Isabel Dominguez.

805
54:30.498 --> 54:32.708
[no audible dialogue]

806
54:32.792 --> 54:34.752
[Jeong-suk] Isabel, you created a dish

807
54:34.835 --> 54:38.005
with one half in a style the younger generation would enjoy

808
54:38.089 --> 54:41.342
and the other half in a style the older generation would enjoy,

809
54:41.425 --> 54:43.969
utilizing a 50-50 concept.

810
54:44.678 --> 54:47.389
We felt that while the food tasted good,

811
54:48.140 --> 54:52.061
it didn't really align with the challenge which was about communication.

812
54:52.144 --> 54:55.439
You merely placed each generation's preferences side by side.

813
54:55.523 --> 54:59.485
But of course, the food was delicious and very well executed.

814
54:59.568 --> 55:01.904
It was a plate with no real flaws.

815
55:01.987 --> 55:04.448
But I'm not sure we can truly call it K-food

816
55:04.532 --> 55:06.826
as it doesn't incorporate any of our cuisine.

817
55:11.956 --> 55:12.957
[no audible dialogue]

818
55:20.756 --> 55:23.217
The Table: K-Chef 2026

819
55:23.300 --> 55:25.469
is a competition to crown the best K-chef.

820
55:26.345 --> 55:27.513
What we want to see

821
55:27.596 --> 55:29.598
is K-food not simply as a craft,

822
55:29.682 --> 55:31.767
but rather as a form of art.

823
55:35.604 --> 55:40.442
All right. Sadly, Isabel must leave the competition this evening.

824
55:40.526 --> 55:41.735
We're sorry to see you go.

825
55:41.819 --> 55:44.029
But we really believe that

826
55:44.113 --> 55:47.533
this experience has helped you grow into an even better chef.

827
55:48.075 --> 55:49.076
Isabel,

828
55:49.160 --> 55:53.372
before you leave us, may we ask you to say a few final words?

829
56:11.015 --> 56:12.975
[light jazz playing]

830
56:27.406 --> 56:28.449
Hey.

831
56:30.868 --> 56:31.869
Hola.

832
56:33.579 --> 56:34.997
Can I buy you a drink?

833
56:35.623 --> 56:36.624
Sure.

834
56:41.086 --> 56:43.881
When I was young, I really didn't like my mother.

835
56:44.465 --> 56:47.843
Like a typical Korean mother, she raised me so strictly.

836
56:47.927 --> 56:50.012
I hated that, so we fought a lot.

837
56:52.223 --> 56:54.058
I wanted to hang out with my friends,

838
56:54.892 --> 56:57.770
listen to music and go out dancing.

839
56:58.687 --> 57:00.940
One day, I ran away from home.

840
57:01.607 --> 57:04.568
I left the countryside and went to the big city, Lima.

841
57:06.654 --> 57:09.156
People were willing to give me work,

842
57:09.240 --> 57:13.619
and I… settled into life in the city without much trouble.

843
57:20.000 --> 57:23.003
But the good times didn't last very long.

844
57:23.087 --> 57:25.923
I was scammed out of the money I'd worked hard to earn.

845
57:27.341 --> 57:28.968
I was betrayed by people I trusted,

846
57:29.051 --> 57:31.762
lost my job and got kicked out of my home.

847
57:31.845 --> 57:36.016
So for several months, I lived like a beggar with no home and no job.

848
57:40.688 --> 57:43.649
One day, I was so weak from hunger that I sat down on the side of the street.

849
57:43.732 --> 57:45.567
[laughing quietly]

850
57:46.318 --> 57:48.112
I must have looked like a beggar.

851
57:48.195 --> 57:50.781
Someone even came and handed me some cash.

852
57:50.864 --> 57:52.658
[both chuckle]

853
57:53.534 --> 57:57.830
[sighs] But it meant a lot to me. It was just enough to buy one meal.

854
58:01.166 --> 58:04.211
That's when I noticed the sign for a Korean restaurant.

855
58:04.795 --> 58:07.798
I went straight in and ordered kimchi jjigae.

856
58:14.680 --> 58:17.391
The stew came out, and I took one spoonful.

857
58:18.100 --> 58:19.101
[sighs]

858
58:23.105 --> 58:25.316
And I started crying like a child.

859
58:26.775 --> 58:29.320
Because I felt my mother in that kimchi jjigae.

860
58:32.489 --> 58:35.576
The kimchi jjigae my mother used to make me when I was little.

861
58:37.119 --> 58:38.746
To me, that was my mom

862
58:40.748 --> 58:42.166
and my country Korea.

863
58:46.337 --> 58:48.589
[gasps, chuckles] Sorry.

864
58:48.672 --> 58:49.882
It's okay.

865
58:50.966 --> 58:52.176
[sniffles]

866
58:55.012 --> 58:58.307
Oh, I'm BTS's biggest fan.

867
58:59.516 --> 59:03.395
They said if I won this competition, they'd open a restaurant for me in Seoul.

868
59:03.479 --> 59:06.482
-[chuckles] -[Isabel laughs]

869
59:08.192 --> 59:11.987
I really wish BTS gave me the honor of trying my food some day.

870
59:13.572 --> 59:14.573
[sniffles]

871
59:16.867 --> 59:20.662
[Captain Cook] The winner of Round One is Team Famiglia!

872
59:20.746 --> 59:22.790
[screams] What! Oh! [laughs]

873
59:22.873 --> 59:24.708
[audience cheering, applauding]

874
59:25.292 --> 59:26.668
[Han] We did it!

875
59:28.253 --> 59:30.381
[Du-yeong] Thank you! Thank you so much!

876
59:31.548 --> 59:33.425
[dramatic music playing]

877
59:44.895 --> 59:47.147
-[mouthing] Thank you. -Oh!

878
59:48.273 --> 59:49.274
Yeah!

879
59:49.358 --> 59:51.652
-[cheering] -[laughs]

880
59:54.321 --> 59:56.532
[pop song playing]

881
59:57.991 --> 59:59.368
Is the potage for Table 5 still not ready?

882
59:59.451 --> 01:00:01.161
It's almost done, Chef! Ready in one more minute!

883
01:00:01.245 --> 01:00:03.664
-Okay. What about the salad? -[Du-yeong] It's almost finished, Chef.

884
01:00:03.747 --> 01:00:05.791
-Let's pick up the pace a bit. -Yes, Chef.

885
01:00:11.630 --> 01:00:13.507
Is the mushroom risotto for Table 6 still not ready?

886
01:00:13.590 --> 01:00:16.343
It's done. I just need to slice the button mushrooms.

887
01:00:18.095 --> 01:00:19.555
Okay.

888
01:00:19.638 --> 01:00:20.681
Wait.

889
01:00:24.643 --> 01:00:25.686
Okay. Go.

890
01:00:28.647 --> 01:00:29.648
[exhales]

891
01:00:31.900 --> 01:00:33.318
-Sorry to keep you waiting. -Thank you.

892
01:00:33.402 --> 01:00:35.779
Oh, thank you. Enjoy your meal.

893
01:00:35.863 --> 01:00:39.408
Excuse me? We ordered a Coke and a Sprite, but we still haven't gotten them.

894
01:00:39.491 --> 01:00:42.286
-We've been waiting ten minutes too. -Oh, yes.

895
01:00:43.370 --> 01:00:44.455
[gasps, screams]

896
01:00:44.538 --> 01:00:45.998
Oh, my. Oh, my.

897
01:00:46.081 --> 01:00:47.791
[diner] I think we need something to wipe this up.

898
01:00:47.875 --> 01:00:49.334
-Are you okay? Oh, my, what do we do? -Yes.

899
01:00:49.418 --> 01:00:50.335
-Are you okay? -Yes.

900
01:00:50.419 --> 01:00:53.213
-Could you bring us some more napkins? -Oh, yes. Just a moment.

901
01:00:54.173 --> 01:00:55.174
[grunts]

902
01:00:55.257 --> 01:00:56.258
Excuse me.

903
01:00:56.341 --> 01:00:58.218
We ordered the steak quite a while ago.

904
01:00:58.302 --> 01:01:00.262
Excuse me, can we get a menu?

905
01:01:00.345 --> 01:01:02.347
-A menu. We've been here over 20 minutes. -[gasps]

906
01:01:02.431 --> 01:01:04.308
-Could you bring us a new fork? -Oh, yes.

907
01:01:04.391 --> 01:01:06.852
-Could we get a new spoon here? -Oh, yes. [grunts]

908
01:01:06.935 --> 01:01:08.604
Could we get some wine over here?

909
01:01:09.271 --> 01:01:10.856
More water, please.

910
01:01:10.939 --> 01:01:15.068
[sighs] Yes, I'm sorry. I'm sorry.

911
01:01:24.870 --> 01:01:27.039
[exhales shakily]

912
01:01:37.090 --> 01:01:38.258
Do you…

913
01:01:38.342 --> 01:01:40.177
Do you know what you've done, huh?

914
01:01:42.137 --> 01:01:43.138
Get it.

915
01:01:44.223 --> 01:01:46.016
Just get it no matter what.

916
01:01:49.394 --> 01:01:50.812
Do whatever it takes to get it.

917
01:01:50.896 --> 01:01:52.064
Or else…

918
01:01:54.816 --> 01:01:56.735
I won't let you get away with this.

919
01:01:56.818 --> 01:01:58.570
Y-Yes, Chef. [breathes shakily]

920
01:01:58.654 --> 01:02:00.322
I'll go get it. I'll figure something out.

921
01:02:00.405 --> 01:02:03.242
I promise I won't let you down. I'm sorry. I'm so sorry.

922
01:02:10.207 --> 01:02:11.833
[emotional music playing]

923
01:02:29.601 --> 01:02:33.188
[Han] Because in the end, you'll lose to me again anyway.

924
01:02:33.272 --> 01:02:35.315
Just like seven years ago.

925
01:02:35.399 --> 01:02:36.400
Chris K.

926
01:02:46.660 --> 01:02:48.412
[door opens]

927
01:02:53.792 --> 01:02:56.587
[sighs] The kitchen's all cleaned up.

928
01:02:57.671 --> 01:02:58.714
Wow…

929
01:02:59.590 --> 01:03:02.175
You two worked so hard covering the dining room too.

930
01:03:06.054 --> 01:03:07.264
[Song-yi groans]

931
01:03:07.806 --> 01:03:10.434
-Oh, my back. [sighs] -[sighs]

932
01:03:10.517 --> 01:03:14.271
Let's just finish this, go inside and get some rest, Auntie.

933
01:03:14.354 --> 01:03:15.689
Yeah, let's do that.

934
01:03:18.984 --> 01:03:21.612
Oh. Ms. Seon, what brings you here at this hour?

935
01:03:21.695 --> 01:03:24.197
Well, the chairwoman mentioned…

936
01:03:24.281 --> 01:03:28.076
she really wanted to try the food from the winner of Round One tonight.

937
01:03:28.160 --> 01:03:29.953
Ever since you made it to the main rounds,

938
01:03:30.037 --> 01:03:32.164
I heard reservations are hard to get,

939
01:03:32.956 --> 01:03:35.500
so we decided to come right around closing.

940
01:03:36.168 --> 01:03:37.753
The chairwoman?

941
01:03:37.836 --> 01:03:39.296
[dramatic music playing]

942
01:03:44.426 --> 01:03:45.802
[pop ballad playing]

943
01:04:26.426 --> 01:04:28.011
[ballad continues]

944
01:05:01.753 --> 01:05:03.004
[Han] If not me…

945
01:05:03.547 --> 01:05:04.881
then who can?

946
01:05:04.965 --> 01:05:06.049
I'll tell them to leave.

947
01:05:06.133 --> 01:05:08.260
[Han] They're customers who came to eat our food.

948
01:05:08.343 --> 01:05:10.554
[Jeong-won] Did you come looking for your birth parents?

949
01:05:10.637 --> 01:05:12.764
There are many reasons I could give.

950
01:05:12.848 --> 01:05:15.142
I just don't know which one is the real reason.

951
01:05:16.601 --> 01:05:18.353
What I care about

952
01:05:18.895 --> 01:05:22.023
is who you've killed in the past.

953
01:05:22.107 --> 01:05:23.859
[Woo-jin] What's the connection between you two?

954
01:05:23.942 --> 01:05:25.652
Why were you sitting in front of

955
01:05:25.736 --> 01:05:27.529
the late Kim Tae-seop?

956
01:05:28.238 --> 01:05:29.364
Not yet.

957
01:05:31.032 --> 01:05:33.160
[ballad continues]
